WEBVTT

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.239
12x smaller than the 9B.

00:00:03.239 --> 00:00:06.294
All four test images came out less faithful.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.858
The pitch, from ML-Intern-lab: We shrank it to 0.8B.

00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:13.229
It fits on a laptop now.

00:00:13.229 --> 00:00:14.020
It holds.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:19.840
Seconds per rewrite, on four CPU cores.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.315
9B teacher, 387 s; 0.8B student, 14.1 s.

00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:27.368
That is 27x.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.394
The release's own image judge picked slot A.

00:00:31.394 --> 00:00:35.648
240 of 240: won by whichever image sat in slot A.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.926
Nine checks the four requests define.

00:00:38.926 --> 00:00:42.093
7 of 9: passed by the raw prompts.

00:00:42.093 --> 00:00:45.260
1 of 9: passed by the 0.8B's rewrites.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.636
One draw per arm, four prompts.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.519
The prompt as typed was the most faithful and the fastest.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.858
Every number here is from the full article, with its sources, at ai.thesatyajit.com.
